    "q-156118": {
      "dom_id": "step-q156118",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the set of values of $z$ that satisfy $1-z>3$ and $2z+1>5.$",
      "worked_solution": "To solve the system, we solve each inequality separately and then find their intersection.\n\n- First, we solve $1-z>3:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-z & >3 \\\\ 1-z-1 & >3-1 \\\\ -z & >2 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-z) & <(-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\ z & <-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194110/assets/72d4febdc48282faebade8f8a4b827a72b82433b054c6409752f892cb718ec85.png)\n\n- Then, we solve $2z+1>5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z+1 & >5 \\\\ 2z+1-1 & >5-1 \\\\ 2z & >4 \\\\ \\frac{2z}{2} & >\\frac{4}{2} \\\\ z & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194110/assets/84893a305567a0aaaafb6fd2393c62f05086780ce8c8a55b91ba68676ba309ce.png)\n\nTo find the values of $z$ that satisfy both inequalities, we find where the two solutions overlap:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194110/assets/04b20be3eaf236e70aa5bdf8cf6e25b6838b3bb823fe16f926112fb1f5e02d6f.png)\n\nIt turns out that the solutions do not overlap at all. Therefore, there are no values of $z$ that satisfy both inequalities simultaneously. So, there is no solution.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
